PRM80 control software
======================

This software is for use with the F4FEZ/F8EGQ/DC0CM PRM80 firmware.
It needs PRM80xx firmware V5.1 or newer (https://github.com/RaspCla/prm80).
Firmware older than V5.0 is refused when connecting. With V5.0 the program
works, but warns: shift reverse, the scan of the PRM80 and the power switch in
VFO mode do not work correctly, a new default shift or scan speed only works
after a restart of the PRM80.

Homepage: https://github.com/RaspCla/prm80-control-software

Since version 2.0 the program runs on Windows and Linux (and should run on macOS):

  - Build system: Maven (pom.xml). NetBeans, IntelliJ IDEA and VS Code open
    the project directly; the libraries are downloaded automatically.
  - Serial port: jSerialComm (replaces RXTX, no separate native libraries)
  - Look and feel: FlatLaf (start with --dark for the dark variant)
  - The old Swing Application Framework, swing-layout and swingworker
    libraries are no longer needed.

Requirements
------------
  - JDK 21 or newer (e.g. Eclipse Temurin, https://adoptium.net)
  - for development: Apache NetBeans (GUI builder for the .form files)

Build and run
-------------
  NetBeans:      File > Open Project > select this folder (control_software/trunk),
                 then "Clean and Build" and "Run".
  Command line:  mvn package
                 java -jar target/prm80-control-software-<version>.jar

The jar in target/ contains all libraries and runs on every computer with Java 21+.
NetBeans "Run" / "Debug" use nbactions.xml (adds --enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED,
needed by FlatLaf and jSerialComm from Java 24 on; java -jar gets it from the manifest).

Stand-alone program (no Java installation needed)
--------------------------------------------------
After building, run

  Windows:  package-windows.bat        -> target\dist\PRM80\PRM80.exe
  Linux:    ./package-linux.sh         -> target/dist/PRM80/bin/PRM80

The PRM80 folder contains its own Java runtime and can be copied or zipped.
"package-windows.bat msi" (needs WiX Toolset) or "./package-linux.sh deb"
build an installer / package instead.

Serial port on Linux
--------------------
The user needs access to the serial ports, usually by membership in the
group "dialout":   sudo usermod -aG dialout $USER   (then log in again)
Ports are named /dev/ttyUSB0, /dev/ttyACM0, /dev/ttyS0, ... on Linux and
COM1, COM2, ... on Windows. Select the port at the top of the main window.

Connecting
----------
Select the serial port in the main window and press "Connect". The list of
ports is updated each time it is opened (e.g. after plugging in a USB adapter).
All communication with the PRM80 runs in the background: the display changes
at once, "BUSY" is shown in the display until the PRM80 has answered.
The state of the PRM80 is read every 1.5 s (changes made at the radio).

Channel manager
---------------
Tools > Channel manager. It shows the channel file used last time.
  Read from PRM80 / Write to PRM80   exchange the channels with the RAM of
                                     the radio (see "RAM and EEPROM")
  Read / Save / Save As              channel file (CSV, separator ";", UTF-8)
  Verify                             compare the list shown with a channel file
"Shift Freq." 0 with "Shift Enabled" means: the channel uses the default shift
of the PRM80 (Configuration, Default shift). A change of the default shift applies to
all these channels at once; another value is the own shift of the channel.
Insert / Delete / Move up / Move down (keys: Insert, Del, Alt+Up, Alt+Down)
change the order of the channels; the following channels get new numbers,
the comments move with them. The PRM80 has no gaps in its channel list (it
only knows the number of channels), so "Write to PRM80" writes the whole list
again. Insert adds a copy of the selected channel, change it as needed.
Undo (Ctrl+Z) takes back the last changes (up to 50), Redo (Ctrl+Y or
Ctrl+Shift+Z) does them again (until the next change); reading a list from the
PRM80 or a file cannot be undone.
Verify compares the list shown channel by channel with a file and lists the
differences (settings and, separately, comments). To check the radio against
a file: "Read from PRM80", then "Verify" with the file.
Several channels can be changed at once: select the rows (Shift + click or
Ctrl + click, the last click may open the value to change), change the value
in one of them, then answer the question "... for all selected channels?"
with Yes (not for the frequency).
The PRM80 has no memory for comments: they are only kept in the file.
The main window shows the comment of the current channel below the channel
number (if the channel in the list has the same frequency as in the PRM80). When the
channels are read from the PRM80, the comments of the list shown before are
kept for channels with the same number and frequency.

"Export A51" (shown in expert mode) saves the list as channel configuration
of the firmware: src/inc_144.a51 or src/inc_430.a51, ready to assemble with
the firmware (make). The firmware loads these channels when RAM and EEPROM are
invalid (first start) or with the "I" command. The file contains
  CONFIG_CHAN_COUNT (highest channel number), default shift, PLL divider,
  scan duration, freq_list, shift_list (shift per channel, firmware V5) and
  chan_state_table (shift enabled, positive, reverse, scan lock out),
with the frequency, shift and comment of every channel as comment.
PLL step, default shift and scan speed are taken from the connected PRM80;
without connection the defaults of the firmware are used (V5, 12.5 kHz,
600 kHz / 7.6 MHz, 400 ms). Frequencies and shifts must be multiples of the
PLL step.

Configuration
-------------
Tools > Configuration: PLL step, default shift, scan speed and squelch
(the same squelch as in the main window). It works like the channel manager:
  Read from PRM80 / Write to PRM80   exchange the values with the RAM of
                                     the radio (see "RAM and EEPROM")
  Read / Save / Save As              configuration file (*.cfg, text)
The values are only sent to the PRM80 with "Write to PRM80"; the dialog shows
whether the values shown differ from those in the PRM80. Save As proposes the
name of the channel list of the channel manager with ".cfg", so a channel
list (CSV) and its configuration (cfg) belong together. A file of another band
is only used after a question.

RAM and EEPROM
--------------
The PRM80 works with the channels and settings in its RAM. The RAM keeps
them for some weeks without power. The EEPROM holds a permanent copy, which
the PRM80 loads when the content of the RAM is lost.
"Read from PRM80" and "Write to PRM80" (channel manager and configuration)
only use the RAM. Tools > Memory:
  Copy RAM -> EEPROM (save permanently)   copies the whole RAM to the EEPROM:
        all channels AND all settings (PLL step, default shift, scan speed,
        squelch, mode, current channel), not only the channel list.
        The previous content of the EEPROM is lost.
  Copy EEPROM -> RAM (restore)            replaces the whole RAM by the
        EEPROM. Save the channel list and the configuration in files first if
        you may need the current state again.
Both ask first and take some seconds. The usual way to change the channels
permanently: Channel manager > Read from PRM80, change, Write to PRM80, then
Tools > Memory > Copy RAM -> EEPROM.

VFO mode
--------
In VFO mode "Shift enabled", "Shift positive", "Shift reverse" and "Shift
Frequency" set the frequencies at once: TX = RX +/- shift, with reverse RX and
TX are swapped (as the firmware does it for channels).
In memory mode the fields only show the shift of the current channel and are
greyed (the shift of a channel is changed in the channel manager). Only
"Shift reverse" can be used in memory mode, as the reverse key of the radio,
if the channel has shift enabled. With firmware V5.2 this reverse ends when
another channel is selected (the reverse stored in the channel is kept), with
V5.1 it is stored in the channel.

"Write to MEM" (VFO mode) stores the VFO setting in a memory channel. The
channel list of the channel manager is used; it is only read from the PRM80
when it is not known to be the same (asks before a different list is
replaced). The dialog stays open: write to further channels, "Undo" takes
writes back, "OK" or "Cancel" close it (written channels stay in the PRM80).
The channel manager shows the changes.

Scan
----
"Scan" starts the scan, pressing it again stops it. Any operation of the radio
in the program (channel +/-, VFO/MEM, entering a channel or frequency, Write
to MEM ...) stops the scan as well; volume, squelch and power do not.
  MEM mode   all memory channels one after the other, channels with
             "scan lock" (as stored in the PRM80, see "Write to PRM80" in
             the channel manager) are skipped without delay; if the list is
             not known to be the one in the PRM80, it is read at the start
             (a few seconds)
  VFO mode   a frequency range with a step: Tools > Scan settings
             (asked for at the first VFO scan, stored separately for 144 and
             430 MHz); the shift fields are applied as in VFO mode
On every channel the scan waits the scan speed of the PRM80 (Tools >
Configuration, time per channel), then reads the squelch. Squelch open: the
scan stays there ("HOLD" in the display) while the signal is received and the
hold time after it (Tools > Scan settings, default 3 s), then continues.
Transmitting stops the scan. The display shows "SCAN" while scanning.
The scan is done by the program (the firmware V5 has no serial command to start
its own scan), so it runs only while the PRM80 is connected. Squelch 0 (always
open) stops the scan on every channel.

Expert mode
-----------
Tools > Expert mode shows the functions for experts (restart,
serial monitor, A51 export in the channel manager, TCP server connection,
virtual PRM for tests).

The virtual PRM (File > Connect virtual PRM (test)) simulates a PRM8060 144 MHz with
firmware V5.2: factory channels of src/inc_144.a51,
shift per channel, reverse, scan lock, default shift, scan duration, RAM with
checksums and EEPROM (restart, RAM <-> EEPROM). Known errors of the
firmware are not simulated (reverse uses the channel in RAM and its own shift,
configuration changes update the checksum).

Zoom
----
The window can be resized with the mouse, the whole user interface zooms
with it. Alternatively: View menu, Ctrl + / Ctrl - / Ctrl 0 (100 %).
The zoom factor is shown in the status bar and remembered for the next start.

Command line options
--------------------
  -h,   --help           Print this help message
  -s,   --server=PORT    Server mode. Listen on TCP port PORT
  -p,   --port=SERIAL    Serial port for server mode (e.g. COM3 or /dev/ttyUSB0),
                         default: the port selected in the GUI options
  -d,   --debug          Set debug mode
        --dark           Use the dark look and feel

History
-------
